    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Walter Powell and Kaisa Snellman define knowledge economy as production and services based on knowledge-intensive activities that contribute to an accelerated pace of technological and scientific advancement, in addition to equally rapid obsolescence. The knowledge economy is based on theoretical knowledge being a source of innovation, and the primary sources of theoretical knowledge in the economy are sites of knowledge production. Academia is one such site. By conducting a content analysis on papers about Intellectual Property and Indigenous Knowledge protection to examine academia’s role in the knowledge economy of colonized African nations, I argue that the globalized form of the knowledge economy manifests as a phenomenon of postcolonial hauntology by creating a neocolonial hell that locks African nations into a model not serving their development needs.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Babalola, T. (2024). “HELL HATH NO THEORY”: THE ABYSS OF EPISTEMIC DISSONANCE IN POSTCOLONIAL AFRICA. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/vh246sp5004. https://doi.org/10.25740/vh246sp5004.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
